<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-master-index">
<title>Master Index</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>None. This section is intended as a navigation reference
for the complete textbook.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this reference section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>locate major mathematical subjects in the textbook;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify relationships among mathematical disciplines;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish foundational, pure, applied, and computational topics;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>navigate from elementary prerequisites to advanced subjects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use the textbook as an interconnected mathematical reference.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-18-master-index-subsec-master-index-use">
<title>How to Use the Master Index</title>
<p>This index organizes major topics alphabetically and directs the reader to
their primary location in the textbook.</p>

<p>A topic may appear in several chapters.</p>

<p>The listed location identifies its principal treatment or the most useful
starting point.</p>

